## Data and operator structure

- K: 222x222 (square), float64; y: 222 observations (y_obs.csv has NO header).
- Singular values decay geometrically: sigma_k ~ 0.8575^k (log-linear slope -0.1538 over the first 100 SVs); sigma_1 = 1.0, sigma_10 = 0.2506, sigma_100 ~ 2.1e-7, trailing SVs ~ 2e-15 (machine precision).
- Condition number ~ 5.7e14: severely ill-posed (exponential spectral decay).
- Numerical rank by tolerance: 90 @1e-6, 150 @1e-10, 180 @1e-12, 195 @1e-13, 210 @1e-14.

## Noise characterization (why the naive view is wrong)

- Naive least-squares residual looks white (Ljung-Box p ~ 0.21), but this is an artifact: the numerically full-rank K absorbs the noise subspace into the solution.
- Truncated-fit residuals are strongly autocorrelated in index space: TSVD(k=100) ACF(1)=0.63 (Ljung-Box p<1e-6); TSVD(k=150) ACF(1)=0.36, ACF(2)=0.29, ACF(10)=0.08 (p<1e-6).
- Decisive spectral test: beta_k = u_k^T y is WHITE across k for k >= 150 (spectral ACF ~ 0), but its variance is NOT flat: the running rms rises from ~0.019 (k~75) to ~0.028 (tail), with a significant bump (~0.033-0.035) around k=105-150. White noise projected on the tail subspace would show ACF(1) ~ -0.02, versus +0.36 observed.
- Conclusion: the noise is COLORED. Its covariance in measurement space is C = U diag(sigma_k^2) U^T with sigma_k growing with k (heteroscedastic across singular components, average variance growth ~1.16x per 10 indices).
- AR(p) index-space models cannot represent this covariance: for p=1..8 the predicted profile sqrt(u_k^T C u_k) is essentially flat (pattern correlation with the observed profile ~0.12; mean 0.0148 vs observed 0.029), and a naive AR(1) fit to the tail-only residual carries only ~half the true noise variance because the fitted components absorb the rest.

## Prewhitening (two stages, iterate-until-stable)

- Stage 1 (primary attempt): AR(4) Toeplitz prewhitening (pilot k=150, AIC order selection, refit-and-reestimate loop, 2 iterations). Validation: whitened tail variance 111 (target 1), log-variance trend slope 0.0004176, whitened-residual Ljung-Box Q(10)=173.01 p=0.0000 -> FAILED.
- Stage 2 (fallback, USED): spectral-diagonal prewhitening W = diag(1/sigma_k) U^T. sigma_k estimated nonparametrically (running rms, window 21) over the noise-dominated region k >= 39, flat conservative extrapolation below; sigma_k re-estimated from the residual of the current whitened fit and iterated to stability (1 iterations, final max relative change 0.0000).
- Validation of the whitened system: whitened tail variance 1.027 (target 1.0), whitened beta tail ACF(1..5) = [-0.131 -0.035 -0.052  0.099 -0.118], log-variance trend slope -0.00054, Ljung-Box on whitened residual of the discrepancy-selected fit: Q(10)=9.73, p=0.4643 -> white (validation passed).

- noise_estimate = 0.0294644: average noise std in measurement space, sqrt(trace(C)/m) with C = U diag(sigma_k^2) U^T the estimated noise covariance. Per-component noise std ranges from ~0.0307 (low k, extrapolated) to ~0.0281 (far tail).

## Picard / effective rank

- Picard table (picard_data.csv): |u_k^T y| drops from ~1.6 (k=0) to the noise floor ~0.02-0.03 within the first ~40 components; beyond the knee the ratio |u_k^T y|/sigma_k explodes, which is the signature of noise, not signal.
- effective_rank = 27 of 222: largest k for which the median-smoothed coefficient |u_k^T y| exceeds the estimated per-component noise level sigma_k. This is consistent with the published Picard table and with noise_estimate = 0.0294644.

## Discrepancy principle check

- In whitened space the noise has (approximately) unit variance, so a fit with d effective parameters should show whitened residual norm ~ sqrt(m - d).
- Achieved: TSVD 13.766 (target 14.000, dev 1.7%), Tikhonov 13.791 (target 14.019, dev 1.6%), CGLS 13.992 (target 14.000, dev 0.1%).
- dpc_judgment: holds (regularized solutions fitting the data exactly to the noise level exist).
